FINDINGS = [
 ("F1","Background","Chapter 24 of the 3rd edition, Text-to-Speech and Speech Recognition Engines, teaches three third-party packages: pyttsx3, which speaks text through the speech engine of the operating system (SAPI5 on Windows, NSSpeechSynthesizer on macOS, eSpeak on Linux) with say(), runAndWait(), getProperty() and setProperty() for rate, volume and voice, and save_to_file() for a WAV file; Whisper, installed as openai-whisper, which loads a model named tiny, base, small, medium or large-v3 with load_model(), transcribes an audio file with transcribe() into a dictionary whose 'text' key holds the words, and writes SRT, VTT, TSV, TXT or JSON files through get_writer(); and yt-dlp, which downloads a video or only its audio or metadata; it closes with seven practice questions and three practice programs: a spoken guess-the-number game, the song 99 Bottles of Beer, and a YouTube transcriber.",["P01"]),
 ("F2","Comparative analysis","The behaviour of pyttsx3 that the chapter describes reproduces where it can be run without a sound card: with the package's dummy driver under Python 3.14.4, say() returns at once and only queues the text, the events started-utterance and finished-utterance come only after runAndWait(), setProperty('rate', 300) takes effect only when the queue is run, getProperty('volume') and getProperty('rate') give 1.0 and 200, getProperty('voices') is a list of Voice objects that print as <pyttsx3.voice.Voice object at 0x...>, and a Voice made without gender, age and languages holds None, None and an empty list, as the chapter says; the default init() on this machine raises RuntimeError saying that eSpeak or eSpeak-ng is probably not installed, which is why the chapter tells Linux readers to install espeak; no speech was heard and no real voice was listed, because the machine has no engine and no sound card.",["P01","P12"]),
 ("F3","Comparative analysis","The chapter's statement that pyttsx3 can save .wav files only holds for two of its three engines and not for the third: the eSpeak driver of version 2.99 writes the file with the standard wave module as mono, 16-bit, 22,050 Hz, the SAPI5 driver writes through a SAPI file stream, and the macOS driver carries the comment that Apple writes .aiff, not .wav; the standard-library modules for AIFF (aifc), audioop, sunau, chunk and sndhdr were removed in Python 3.13 by PEP 594 and are absent under 3.14.4, while wave remains, so a WAV file can be read with the standard library and an AIFF file cannot.",["P01","P02","P08","P09","P12"]),
 ("F4","Comparative analysis","The chapter's account of Whisper holds with additions the chapter leaves out: release 20250625 accepts fourteen model names, not five, among them the .en models, large-v1, large-v2, large, and turbo with large-v3-turbo, and its command line uses turbo as the default model where the chapter recommends base; the five names the chapter gives are all valid; Whisper decodes every audio file by starting the ffmpeg command-line program for 16 kHz mono samples, so ffmpeg must be installed, which the chapter does not say; the memory column of the chapter's Table 24-1 (1, 1, 2, 5 and 10 GB) equals the required-VRAM column of the package's README; the runtimes of the table were measured by the author on his laptop and were not reproduced, since no model can be downloaded and run here, and the only numbers computed from them are ratios, such as the 'large-v3' model taking 12.8 times as long as 'base' on the 15-minute sample; the package lists Python 3.8 to 3.13 and was not installed or run here under Python 3.14.4.",["P01","P15","P16"]),
 ("F5","Comparative analysis","The chapter's sample subtitle files reproduce when Whisper's own writer source is executed: the SRT file numbers each block from 1, always shows hours and uses a comma before the milliseconds (00:00:05,640), the VTT file starts with the line WEBVTT and shows hours only when they are not zero (00:05.640), the TSV file has the header line start, end and text with times as integer milliseconds (5640), and the TXT file has one stripped segment per line; the writer replaces --> inside a segment's text by ->, names the output file after the audio file without its folder or extension (talks/hello.wav gives hello.srt), and raises KeyError for a format it does not know; the W3C WebVTT text confirms that hours may be omitted when zero, and the description of SubRip confirms the comma.",["P01","P15","P19","P20"]),
 ("F6","Comparative analysis","The chapter's language option holds and is wider than the chapter says: get_tokenizer lower-cases the name, so language='English' is accepted, the package knows 100 languages, and the chapter's statement that the engines work with most widely spoken languages is consistent with that list; the dictionary returned by transcribe() has exactly the keys text, segments and language, and each segment carries start, end and text, which is what the subtitle writers read; with word timestamps requested, each segment also carries a list of words, and the writers can then break subtitles by a number of words per line or by a line width and a line count, options the chapter does not cover.",["P01","P15"]),
 ("F7","Comparative analysis","The chapter's yt-dlp code is consistent with the package, checked offline in a scratch environment with yt-dlp 2026.8.19 under Python 3.14.4 without any download: the template 'downloaded_content.%(ext)s' gives downloaded_content.m4a, the post-processor FFmpegExtractAudio exists and needs ffmpeg, and sanitize_info() exists; but the chapter's prose names ydl.sanitize(), which the package does not have, the download() method also accepts a single address instead of a list because it wraps it, extract_info() downloads by default unless skip_download is set as the chapter does, and a title is not stripped of its forbidden characters as the chapter says but has them replaced by look-alike characters such as a full-width question mark and colon; no video was fetched, because the session has no route to the video sites.",["P01","P17","P18"]),
 ("F8","Contemporary developments","Current practice adds what the chapter does not show: pyttsx3 2.99 has a module-level speak() that calls init(), say() and runAndWait(), a dummy driver that lets programs be tested without a voice, callbacks registered with connect() for the events started-utterance, started-word, finished-utterance and error, and say() returns an error message instead of queuing an empty string; its Read the Docs page still describes version 2.6 from 2021; programs that speak or transcribe are best written so that the engine is passed in, as the practice programs here do with recording stand-ins, and every result of Whisper needs human review, as the chapter says.",["P01","P12","P13","P14"]),
 ("F9","Conclusion","For an advanced course, chapter 24 is best taught as glue code around three engines that cannot all run in a classroom: what runs offline is the standard-library part (wave files, subtitle formats, file names, JSON, glob, arithmetic on the book's table) and the package logic that has a dummy mode (pyttsx3's queue, properties and events), and what needs a voice, a model, ffmpeg or a network (speaking, saving with a real engine, load_model, transcribe, yt-dlp downloads) is read and not executed; the chapter's printed behaviour holds, and the course should add the AIFF case and the removed audio modules, the fourteen Whisper model names and the turbo default, the ffmpeg requirement, the word-level subtitle options, yt-dlp's look-alike file names, and the testing of speech code with injected engines.",["P01","P08","P12","P15","P17"]),
]
